## Nail Colours for Fair Skin by Season

Seasons matter more for fair skin than for any other skin tone, partly because fair complexions shift slightly across the year  winter skin is paler and cooler-looking, summer skin picks up whatever warmth it can  and partly because the light conditions change what colours read like.

**Spring** is the natural home of pastel on fair skin, but the mistake is going too light. A baby pink that sits close to your natural skin tone in winter natural light looks even more invisible in the softer light of spring. Lean slightly deeper: a dusty rose rather than a barely-there pink, a muted lavender rather than an icy lilac, a pale mint rather than a white-green. These have just enough pigment to read in spring light without tipping into summer boldness.

**Summer** is when fair skin can carry coral and bright pink most confidently. The warmth in the air and the slight colour in the skin from sun exposure means that warm, saturated shades read vibrant rather than jarring. Coral is one of the most consistently flattering summer shades for both cool and warm-toned fair skin  it adds warmth without orange, and it photographs well in natural light.

**Autumn** is burgundy season, and fair skin wears it better than almost any other skin tone. The richness of the colour against pale hands is pure contrast dressing  it looks intentional, editorial, and somehow exactly right. Berry, deep plum, and warm terracotta all belong here.

**Winter** is when fair skin and dark, jewel-toned polish make the most sense. Emerald, sapphire blue, deep cranberry, and metallic chrome all read richer against winter-pale skin than at any other point in the year. This is also when silver and chrome finishes shine  cool metallics sit beautifully against pale winter skin in a way that gold cannot quite replicate.

